––– comment –––
Regression for #4044: cutoff applies to full-query matches, not to a single
include filter's SecondaryIndex candidate set.
https://github.com/manticoresoftware/manticoresearch/issues/4044

The matching row has the ninth city_id. With one attribute filter and cutoff=1,
the SecondaryIndex must not discard it before MATCH('main') is intersected.
––– input –––
export SEARCHD_ARGS="--force-pseudo-sharding"
––– output –––
––– block: ../base/start-searchd –––
––– input –––
mysql -P9306 -h0 -e "DROP TABLE IF EXISTS gh4044; CREATE TABLE gh4044 (street text, city_id int); INSERT INTO gh4044 (street,city_id) VALUES ('pine',101),('lake',102),('hill',103),('park',104),('mill',105),('ford',106),('dale',107),('wood',108),('main',109); FLUSH RAMCHUNK gh4044;" && echo ready
––– output –––
ready
––– input –––
mysql -P9306 -h0 -e "SELECT street,city_id FROM gh4044 WHERE MATCH('main') AND city_id IN (101,102,103,104,105,106,107,108,109) OPTION cutoff=1 /*+ SecondaryIndex(city_id) */;"
––– output –––
+--------+---------+
| street | city_id |
+--------+---------+
| main   |     109 |
+--------+---------+
––– comment –––
A second filter causes the old iterator selection to drop cutoff. It is a
control, not the bug contract: both equivalent predicates must find row 9.
––– input –––
mysql -P9306 -h0 -e "SELECT street,city_id FROM gh4044 WHERE MATCH('main') AND (1=1 AND city_id IN (101,102,103,104,105,106,107,108,109)) OPTION cutoff=1 /*+ SecondaryIndex(city_id) */;"
––– output –––
+--------+---------+
| street | city_id |
+--------+---------+
| main   |     109 |
+--------+---------+
––– comment –––
The same with a KNN iterator: an explicit cutoff must not truncate the
SecondaryIndex candidates before they are intersected with the KNN candidates.
Without the index the query returns a row, so it must with the index too.
––– input –––
mysql -P9306 -h0 -e "DROP TABLE IF EXISTS gh4044_knn; CREATE TABLE gh4044_knn (city_id int, emb float_vector knn_type='hnsw' knn_dims='2' hnsw_similarity='l2'); INSERT INTO gh4044_knn (id,city_id,emb) VALUES $(for i in $(seq 1 99); do printf "(%d,%d,(%d,%d))," $i $((100+i%50)) $((10+i)) $((10+i)); done)(100,149,(0,0)); FLUSH RAMCHUNK gh4044_knn;" && echo ready
––– output –––
ready
––– input –––
for h in NO_SecondaryIndex SecondaryIndex; do mysql -P9306 -h0 -e "SELECT id,city_id FROM gh4044_knn WHERE KNN(emb,1,(0,0)) AND city_id IN ($(seq -s, 100 149)) OPTION cutoff=1 /*+ $h(city_id) */; SHOW META LIKE 'total_found';"; done
––– output –––
+------+---------+
| id   | city_id |
+------+---------+
|    1 |     101 |
+------+---------+
+---------------+-------+
| Variable_name | Value |
+---------------+-------+
| total_found   | 1     |
+---------------+-------+
+------+---------+
| id   | city_id |
+------+---------+
|    1 |     101 |
+------+---------+
+---------------+-------+
| Variable_name | Value |
+---------------+-------+
| total_found   | 1     |
+---------------+-------+
––– comment –––
Deleted rows are dropped after the iterator. With LIMIT and no ORDER BY the
implicit cutoff equals the limit, and it must count live rows: the first 20
rows are deleted, five live rows must come back with one or two indexes.
––– input –––
mysql -P9306 -h0 -e "DROP TABLE IF EXISTS gh4044_del; CREATE TABLE gh4044_del (city_id int, g int); INSERT INTO gh4044_del (id,city_id,g) VALUES $(for i in $(seq 1 199); do printf "(%d,%d,%d)," $i $((100+i%50)) $((i%20)); done)(200,100,0); FLUSH RAMCHUNK gh4044_del; DELETE FROM gh4044_del WHERE id<=20;" && echo ready
––– output –––
ready
––– input –––
mysql -P9306 -h0 -N -e "SELECT id FROM gh4044_del WHERE city_id IN ($(seq -s, 100 149)) LIMIT 5 /*+ SecondaryIndex(city_id) */;" | tr -d '|+-' | wc -w; mysql -P9306 -h0 -N -e "SELECT id FROM gh4044_del WHERE city_id IN ($(seq -s, 100 149)) AND g IN ($(seq -s, 0 19)) LIMIT 5 /*+ SecondaryIndex(city_id,g) */;" | tr -d '|+-' | wc -w
––– output –––
5
5
––– comment –––
A join drops rows of the left table that have no pair, after the iterator:
only ids above 100 have a pair, so the implicit cutoff must not stop the
SecondaryIndex at the first five rows.
––– input –––
mysql -P9306 -h0 -e "DROP TABLE IF EXISTS gh4044_l; DROP TABLE IF EXISTS gh4044_r; CREATE TABLE gh4044_l (city_id int); CREATE TABLE gh4044_r (flag int); INSERT INTO gh4044_l (id,city_id) VALUES $(for i in $(seq 1 199); do printf "(%d,%d)," $i $((100+i%50)); done)(200,100); INSERT INTO gh4044_r (id,flag) VALUES $(for i in $(seq 101 199); do printf "(%d,1)," $i; done)(200,1); FLUSH RAMCHUNK gh4044_l; FLUSH RAMCHUNK gh4044_r;" && echo ready
––– output –––
ready
––– input –––
mysql -P9306 -h0 -N -e "SELECT gh4044_l.id FROM gh4044_l INNER JOIN gh4044_r ON gh4044_l.id=gh4044_r.id WHERE gh4044_l.city_id IN ($(seq -s, 100 149)) LIMIT 5 /*+ SecondaryIndex(city_id) */;" | tr -d '|+-' | wc -w
––– output –––
5
––– comment –––
A brute-force KNN drops rows without a vector after the iterator: the first
100 rows have none, so cutoff=1 must still find a row with a vector.
––– input –––
mysql -P9306 -h0 -e "DROP TABLE IF EXISTS gh4044_nv; CREATE TABLE gh4044_nv (city_id int, emb float_vector knn_type='hnsw' knn_dims='2' hnsw_similarity='l2'); INSERT INTO gh4044_nv (id,city_id) VALUES $(for i in $(seq 1 99); do printf "(%d,%d)," $i $((100+i%50)); done)(100,100); INSERT INTO gh4044_nv (id,city_id,emb) VALUES $(for i in $(seq 101 199); do printf "(%d,%d,(%d,%d))," $i $((100+i%50)) $i $i; done)(200,100,(200,200)); FLUSH RAMCHUNK gh4044_nv;" && echo ready
––– output –––
ready
––– input –––
mysql -P9306 -h0 -N -e "SELECT id FROM gh4044_nv WHERE KNN(emb,5,(0,0),{fullscan=1}) AND city_id IN ($(seq -s, 100 149)) OPTION cutoff=1 /*+ SecondaryIndex(city_id) */;" | tr -d '|+-' | xargs
––– output –––
101
––– comment –––
An IN list with more than eight values and a range are collected into a bitmap
value by value. The cutoff must keep every collected row: no rows are deleted
here, so LIMIT N must return N rows. Each query prints the row count and the
count of rows outside the filter.
––– input –––
mysql -P9306 -h0 -e "DROP TABLE IF EXISTS gh4044_bm; CREATE TABLE gh4044_bm (v int); INSERT INTO gh4044_bm (id,v) VALUES $(for i in $(seq 1 199); do printf "(%d,%d)," $i $((100+i%50)); done)(200,100); FLUSH RAMCHUNK gh4044_bm;" && echo ready
––– output –––
ready
––– input –––
for q in "v IN ($(seq -s, 100 149)) LIMIT 5" "v IN ($(seq -s, 100 149)) LIMIT 30" "v BETWEEN 141 AND 149 LIMIT 5"; do mysql -P9306 -h0 -N -e "SELECT v FROM gh4044_bm WHERE $q /*+ SecondaryIndex(v) */;" | tr -d '|+-' | awk 'NF{n++} END{print n+0}'; done
––– output –––
5
30
5
––– comment –––
An excluded range is inverted after all values are collected, so it must not
get a cutoff: no row with v <= 140 may come back.
––– input –––
for l in 5 100; do mysql -P9306 -h0 -N -e "SELECT v FROM gh4044_bm WHERE v NOT BETWEEN 100 AND 140 LIMIT $l /*+ SecondaryIndex(v) */;" | tr -d '|+-' | awk 'NF{n++; if ($1<=140) bad++} END{print n+0, bad+0}'; done
––– output –––
5 0
36 0
